changes:
  - path: docs/decisions/dec-39-motion-informs-bans-decoration-not-progress.html
    tier: T2
    action: added
    distills: >-
      The reading of motion-informs that this WU settled, written because review round 6 found the
      reasoning had nowhere to live. Design runs 10 and 11 both receipted the slug as a ban on spinners -
      "never a spinner or a bar", then "nothing animates" - and both went further than the rule they were
      applying, whose text bans animation that DECORATES and whose reviewer question is "what does this
      animation tell the user?". The record walks the rule's three clauses against a progress ring: the
      state-change clause passes (a running transfer is a state and the ring is the only thing reporting
      it continuously), the decoration clause is not engaged (it is conditional, and its own DON'T is
      hover glow and panels sliding for style), and the ~2 s expiry clause is not engaged (it governs an
      acknowledgement that must not linger, whereas a progress indicator's duration belongs to the
      transfer - a ring that expired mid-export would report the opposite of the truth). What the runs got
      right is kept as a constraint on any in-flight indicator: it may turn, it may not move anything -
      run 11's "nothing moves" stands in full - and it is never the only channel, a word naming what is
      awaited beside it and the ring aria-hidden. Scope is stated explicitly: this settles the slug's
      READING, not any screen's design; a run may still choose a word alone, it may no longer cite
      motion-informs as the reason it had no choice. The rulebook text is deliberately unchanged, because
      nothing in it was wrong - two applications of it were. Rejected alternatives recorded: editing the
      rulebook, and deleting the runs' original wording.
