  rather than restated in T2.
amendments: >-
  Two review rounds amended two of the docs above after they were first written, and both amendments are
  corrections of this WU's own claims rather than new synthesis. docs/use-cases/web-ui.html: the UC-4 block
  described design run 11 as "archived", which it is not - a run stays in docs/work/ until the epic's
  cleanup (DEC-28), and the plain-text "archived" idiom was minted in d475bef specifically to REPLACE a
  live link once E-REDESIGN's run was really git rm'd, so it asserts git-tier removal. The claim is struck;
  the neighbouring line 79 keeps the idiom correctly because its target genuinely is gone from git.
  docs/work/2026-09-08-export-w6-controls-build-plan.html (v2): its api.ts row and its refusal contract-call
  both said only importHar/importCurl/clearFlows carry the server's sentence verbatim. That was wrong
  against the design the plan itself cites - run 11's Interaction table says all three failures carry the
  server's reason and spells out the export row - so the code changed and the plan says what the design
  says. docs/work/2026-09-08-export-w6-controls-build-result.html (v3) gained a "The review rounds" section
  covering both rounds, the four refuted-in-round-1 lines, the one refutation not accepted and why, and a
  corrected per-suite test count.
  A third amendment to docs/use-cases/web-ui.html followed the manual end-to-end session and the last
  commit, and unlike the first two it IS new synthesis rather than a correction: UC-4's controls block now
  states three facts about the surface that were not true when it was written. The slot's in-flight line is
  joined by a turning ring, and a second ring turns inside the menu in place of the running act's own face,
  because reopening the menu mid-transfer is the one way to ask why an act is withheld. Each act wears a
  face and none of the three is minted for the menu - the save glyph the app already spends on writing
  something out, the very mark an imported row carries in the list below, and the erase the third act is -
  in a box drawn whether or not it is filled, so every label hangs off one left edge and no face reaches
  the accessible name. And the import ask is described as the unequal thing it is: the paste offers the
  file, not the reverse, so the words lead with the command in both places, and the ask holds that command
  READ-ONLY rather than disabled while the import runs, because the target is what the ask opened focused
  on and disabling it drops focus to <body>, outside the dialog, taking Esc with it. That last sentence is
  the doc recording a defect's fix as a property of the surface rather than as a changelog entry, which is
  the tier rule: the use case says what a user gets, and what they get is an import they can still escape.
  A fourth and fifth amendment, both to design runs rather than to T2, followed review round 6. Runs 10
  and 11 each had their motion-informs receipt corrected: the original wording is KEPT verbatim inside the
  new text and marked OVERTURNED, naming the half that survives ("nothing moves" stands, "nothing
  animates" goes) and pointing at DEC-39. Amending a run is not a liberty taken here - run 11 is run 10's
  chartered successor, with an explicit duty per the epic plan to say which of its predecessor's answers
  it keeps and which it overturns, and DEC-29 treats an amendment as re-entering Tier-3 on the merge that
  lands it; round 7 confirmed both preserve rather than erase. The receipts first pointed at the design
  file, which the same WU then retired, leaving two dangling references - round 6 caught that, and they
  now point at DEC-39, which is a record that persists. One process note belongs here rather than being
  discovered later: run 11 could not be snapshotted before editing, the htmldoc server answering 413
  request entity too large, so its before-state is reachable through git rather than through the doc's
  own version picker. Run 10 snapshotted normally.
